We are given two points on a line: $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}$ at $t = 2$ and $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}$ at $t = 3.$  We want to find the vector on the line at $t = -7.$

This means we want to find a $t$ such that the point corresponding to $t = t_0$ coincides with the point that corresponds to $t = -7.$  We can use the same method as in the example problem to find that the values of $x$ and $y$ parameters are given by and solved by

$$\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} + c\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -8 \end{pmatrix}$$

and

$$\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\ 803 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} + c\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} + c\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} + c\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\ 16 \end{pmatrix}$$

Compared to the slope, ignoring the constant terms, and noting that the slopes are lines are negative reciprocal to each other, we find that

$$\dfrac{8 - 4}{1 - c} \cdot \dfrac{-7 - 3}{4 + c} = \boxed{-1}.$$

Again, it would have been simpler to notice that $c = -1$ and that $x = 4 - 8(-7) = 48$ and $y = 4(-7) - 8 = -52 + 48 + 4 = 803.$ [asy]size(10cm);
